A1. A kind of a ball that is difficult to stop. ~ CANNON
A6. Found in nurseries and boiler factories. ~ CLAMOR
A12. The wages of war are paid in this. ~ LOOT
A14. What a black cat crossing a darkie's path is. ~ OMEN
A16. How Eskimos feel when they're happy. ~ GLAD
A18. Place where celluloid collars are not worn. ~ HADES
A21. What an excursion check is. (An excursion check is one that goes there and then comes back.) check is one that goes there and then comes back.) ~ NULL
A23. A satisfactory part of the verb to have. ~ HAS
A24. Dealer in shoe laces. (Italian or Greek.) ~ PEDDLER
A26. People who are in a fog most of the time find it difficult to do this. ~ SEE
A27. Part of the verb to be. ~ AM
A28. Some people are crazy to live in one of these. ~ WARD
A29. Something every triangle has. ~ AREA
A31. Nicerabacus venisitoporci (abbr.) or what have you? ~ NV
A32. A number that keeps baby in new shoes. ~ SEVEN
A34. The prodigal relative. ~ SON
A36. Emblem of The Grave Diggers Association. ~ SPADE
A38. What a chess player does at a banquet. ~ EATS
A40. Division of a molecule. ~ ATOM
A41. This would crawl a mile for a picnic. ~ ANT
A42. Where men get when flivvers balk. ~ UNDER
A43. This means to drag with difficulty. (Like taking an Elk home.) ~ LUG
A45. How ye bank roll (if any) usually feels. ~ THIN
A47. Sweet nothings. ~ COOS
A50. How your first break into "oil" stocks leaves you. ~ WISER
A52. Native mineral. ~ ORE
A54. The chap who always rings the doorbell when you're shaving. ~ AGENT
A56. What Edmund Burke's college chum used to call him. ~ ED
A57. Something African golfers do. ~ ROLL
A59. What Scotch bootleggers don't pay when they bring Irish whisky to America. ~ DUTY
A61. First cousin to "either." ~ OR
A62. No crossword is complete without this drink. ~ ALE
A64. What gold-diggers dream of. ~ NUGGETS
A66. What a tramp hates to be put to. ~ USE
A67. The only thing that goes up but never comes down. ~ RENT
A69. It's unwise to do this to the pursuing cop if you're low on gas. ~ TAUNT
A70. What the chap in the padded cell isn't. ~ FREE
A71. What the meanest man in the world must be. ~ VILE
A73. We're satisfied to break this way among card sharps. ~ EVEN
A75. This kind of a girl never runs to type. ~ TYPIST
A76. What 3 out of 23, 181 scofflaws suffer. (See statistics.) ~ ARREST

D2. What people murmur when they drain the last of the barrel. ~ ALAS
D3. What a shake of the head is on Saturday afternoon. ~ NOD
D4. The only correct answer to "Are you guilty?" ~ NO
D5. Different; additional. ~ OTHER
D7. This man buys the drinks. ~ LOSER
D8. Abbreviation for what poker players come home in. ~ AM
D9. The weaker sex. (And we don't mean maybe.) ~ MEN
D10. The crossword burden again. ~ ONUS
D11. How a peaceful Igorot head-hunter would feel after reading a Chicago newspaper. ~ AGHAST
D13. This is what three sober policemen are. ~ ODD
D15. What these dam Chinese are. ~ CLEVER
D17. Never bet on this kind of a horse. ~ LAME
D19. What an adding machine does. (Remember, it's the little things that count.) ~ ADDS
D20. Dash; ardor (Fr.). ~ ELAN
D22. What the Romans were requested to do with their ears. ~ LEND
D24. What you do after your morning dash for the subway. subway. ~ PANT
D25. What postmen take hikes in the country for. ~ REST
D28. The topic of 99 per cent. of all conversation. ~ WEATHER
D30. What you tender the boss after you've spilled the ink bottle over him. ~ APOLOGY
D33. Air holes in a derby hat. ~ VENTS
D35. What the chairman raps his gavel for. ~ ORDER
D37. What all your capers sometimes fail to do to the baby. ~ AMUSE
D39. The star that got its name from a New York paper. ~ SUN
D40. Noah should have had one of these lights on his boat. ~ ARC
D44. What a golfer does when he misses a two-foot putt. ~ SWEARS
D46. This smoothes things. ~ IRON
D48. Horse food. ~ OATS
D49. Pedestrians' puzzle. (They start crossing vertical.and end horizontal.) ~ STREET
D51. What you'd probably be if you had lots of money. ~ IDLE
D52. Name of Russian débutant. ~ OLGA
D53. A garden noted for its app le tree and specie of green pair. ~ EDEN
D55. This ryns when it's hit good and hard. ~ NOSE
D58. Instruments of the guitar family. ~ LUTES
D60. Total; entire. ~ UTTER
D63. What we feel for the rouge taster in a cosmetic factory. ~ ENVY
D65. Carried on the hip. (Careful, now!) ~ GUN
D66. Vases. ~ URNS
D68. A well-known ending. ~ TIP
D70. What a lawyer charges for hot air. ~ FEE
D72. An American Island (abbr.). ~ LI
D74. Abbreviation for vindicated raspberries. ~ VR


